---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';

const page = codexPage('double-spending');
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    A digital note is just data, and data can be copied. If nothing stopped it, you could pay two people with the same note. The Bitcoin
    whitepaper calls this the <strong>double-spending problem</strong> and solves it with a public chain of transactions that everyone
    checks.<Cite page={page} s="whitepaper" /> Chaum’s ecash solves it differently: the mint remembers every note that has been spent.<Cite page={page} s="chaum" />
  </p>

  <h2>What guardians check</h2>
  <p>When a note is spent, every guardian runs the same checks before accepting it:<Cite page={page} s="fmMintInput" /></p>
  <ol>
    <li>The signature is valid for the note’s denomination under the federation’s key. A forged or mismatched seal fails here.</li>
    <li>The note’s serial is not already in the spent-note ledger.<Cite page={page} s="fmSpentIndex" /></li>
    <li>If both pass, the serial is added to the ledger, so the next attempt fails.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Guardians agree on the order of transactions before they process any of them. If two payments use the same note at the same
    moment, one is always first, and the second finds the serial already spent.
  </p>

  <h2>Receiving notes safely</h2>
  <p>
    A note someone sends you might be a copy they already spent. So a wallet that receives notes swaps them with the federation for fresh
    ones straight away. If the swap succeeds, the notes were good, and nobody else holds a copy of the new ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy of the ledger</h2>
  <p>
    The spent-note ledger holds serials the mint saw for the first time at spending. Because notes were signed blind, a serial in the
    ledger cannot be traced back to the deposit that created it.
  </p>
</Article>
